==Trivia==
* Bug Catcher's sprite from the main series [[Generation II]] games resembles the class' artwork from {{game|Yellow}}.
* The first Bug Catchers battled in all [[Generation III]] games are named Rick.
* In Pokémon Black 2 and White 2, a show on the <sc>Variety</sc> channel mentions that [[Aaron]] used to be a Bug Catcher and a [[Gym Leader]] before joining the [[Elite Four]].
* A Bug Catcher in the {{gdis|Battle Tower|II|Johto Battle Tower}} is named Tajiri; this could be possibly a reference to the creator of the Pokémon series, [[Satoshi Tajiri]], who liked bug-catching as a child.
* In Pokémon Emerald, Bug Catcher Davis on {{rt|123|Hoenn}} has the overworld sprite of a {{tc|Youngster}}.
* Bug Catcher Abner in Pokémon HeartGold and SoulSilver is the only [[core series]] Bug Catcher outside of a [[battle facility]] who uses a non-Bug-type Pokémon, specifically {{p|Pikachu}}.
* In [[Pokémon GO]], "Bug Catcher" is the name of an achievement {{GO|medal}} awarded for catching at least 10 {{type|Bug}} Pokémon.
* In [[Pokémon Champions]], the title of "Bug Catcher" is awarded for winning ten battles using {{type|Bug}} Pokémon.
* The color scheme and outfit of [[Master Trainer]] Bug Catchers in {{LGPE}} strongly resembles the character {{wp|Monkey D. Luffy}} from ''{{wp|One Piece (1999 TV series)|One Piece}}''.

